  "notes": "West leads the ♠Q against 6♣. West's lead is won by South. It appears that the contract depends on either the heart finesse or the diamond finesse working. South can give himself a slight extra chance by playing on elimination lines.\n\nSouth should begin by cashing the other top spade and cashing a trump in hand. A second trump is played to dummy and, when both defenders follow, dummy's last spade is ruffed. South next cashes the top diamonds. When West drops the ♦Q, South's extra chance has come home and the heart finesse can be taken for an overtrick.\n\nIf the ♦Q does not appear, South exits with the ♦J. If East wins the trick (so that the diamond finesse would have worked), he will be endplayed. A heart lead will remove South's possible heart loser. A spade or diamond return will concede a ruff and discard.\n\nIf West wins the ♦Q, South will have to depend on the heart finesse.",
